

[38;5;231m⏺[39m Next — how you actually turn logging up in production. This one matters more than it looks:

  [1mThe fork:[0m with console-only logging, the entire debugging workflow is "get the affected user to raise verbosity,
  reproduce, and send you the console output." So whether the level is runtime-adjustable isn't a nicety — it's the
  mechanism. Also worth naming: this app has no build step and no environment concept, so there is no "production vs
  dev" distinction available to switch on automatically.

  - [1mFixed level, always on[0m — one hardcoded threshold. Simplest. But it's the wrong level in both directions: noisy
    enough to annoy in normal use, or too quiet to help when you need it, and you can't change it without a deploy.
  - [1mRuntime override via [38;5;153mlocalStorage[0m — default to [38;5;153minfo[39m; read a [38;5;153mlogLevel[39m key on startup so you can tell a user to paste
    one line in devtools and reproduce. Persists across reloads, which matters when the bug needs a few navigations to
    trigger.
  - [1mRuntime override via URL param[0m — [38;5;153m?logLevel=debug[39m. Easier to hand to a non-technical user as a link, but it doesn't
    survive navigation and it's visible/shareable in a way that puts verbose logging one copy-pasted URL away from
    spreading.

  [1mMy recommendation: default [38;5;153minfo[39m, overridable via [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m, reading the URL param as a convenience that writes into
[0m  [1m[38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m.[0m The two aren't really competing — the param is how you hand the switch to a user, [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m is what
  makes it stick through the reproduction. It's about six lines together, and skipping it leaves you with logging you
  can't adjust on the machine where the bug actually happens.
